            <ArticleTitle>Transdermal fentanyl therapy for pains in children with infantile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>175-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We used infantile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis (INCL), in which deterioration of the central nervous system is extremely rapid, to study constant release of an opioid for pains of central origin in a metabolic disease. The effect of a transdermal fentanyl patch was studied in five children with INCL. In two of them, measurements of 17 fentanyl serum concentrations and also visual analogue pain scale were obtained during a 15-day study period. Low doses of transdermal fentanyl usually provided good pain relief for the first two days, but not for the third day, of the three-day patch change interval. Pain relief of this type seems mandatory for pains mostly of central origin.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
